---
description: Require trailing commas in multiline comma-separated constructs
alwaysApply: true
---

# Trailing Commas

Always include a trailing comma in multiline comma-separated constructs wherever the language allows it. Single-line constructs do not need trailing commas.

## Apply To

- Function, initializer, method, closure, and generic parameter lists
- Argument lists and call sites
- Array and dictionary literals
- Enum cases with associated values
- Tuple literals and tuple types
- Multiline declarations or builder-style DSL blocks when comma syntax is used

## Examples

```swift
// ❌ BAD — multiline without trailing comma
func configure(
    name: String,
    mode: OperatingMode
) { }

let values = [
    1,
    2,
    3
]

let settings: [String: Int] = [
    "a": 1,
    "b": 2
]

// ✅ GOOD — trailing comma on every multiline element
func configure(
    name: String,
    mode: OperatingMode,
) { }

let values = [
    1,
    2,
    3,
]

let settings: [String: Int] = [
    "a": 1,
    "b": 2,
]

// ✅ GOOD — single-line, no trailing comma required
let compact = [1, 2, 3]
```

When editing existing multiline lists, add trailing commas as part of the change rather than leaving mixed formatting.
